401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (                   Genomics has entered an era of unprecedented scale, leaving us awash in sequence data but often unable to take full advantage of what is already available. Public sequencing databases are a genomic goldmine, from the vast archive of raw reads in the Sequence Read Archive to Logan, which assembles millions of sequencing datasets, or AllTheBacteria, which organises millions of standardized bacterial genomes. Algorithms and data structures are critical to unlocking this wealth of data, making it possible to search, compare, and extract relevant information from massive sequence collections. This keynote therefore explores sequence analysis from the most computationally expensive approaches to the most scalable. We will progress from alignment and mapping to comparisons based on shared k-mers, then examine how minimizers reduce the sequence content retained and how sketching and indexing enable searches across massive collections. The presentation develops a high-level understanding of the ideas behind these methods and the trade-offs they offer. We will examine what each approach can accomplish, its cost in time and memory, and the information it preserves or discards.  Understanding these tradeoffs allows us to match the method to the biological question, avoiding both expensive analyses that add no insight and fast ones that cannot provide an answer.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
