462 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 158 0 R
/T (                Emergence of antibiotic resistant bacteria is a pressing issue in modern day medicine. To fight it and develop new drugs for new resistance mechanisms we have to detect the genetic cause of the resistance. Interpretable artificial intelligence models that predict the resistance phenotype from the full genome sequences are ideal for the task. To train these models, I use a genomic LLM, Evo, to produce embeddings from the genomic sequences. I embedded thousands of P. aeruginosa genomes from the Bakta database that were tested for different antibiotics, and trained classifiers to predict the resistance phenotype using these embeddings. By training a Positive-Unlabeled classifier on genomic windows, where windows from susceptible strains were labeled positive and windows from resistant strains were unlabeled, I was able to identify the windows harboring the relevant genes in the resistant strains.)
/E (                Emergence of antibiotic resistant bacteria is a pressing issue in modern day medicine. To fight it and develop new drugs for new resistance mechanisms we have to detect the genetic cause of the resistance. Interpretable artificial intelligence models that predict the resistance phenotype from the full genome sequences are ideal for the task. To train these models, I use a genomic LLM, Evo, to produce embeddings from the genomic sequences. I embedded thousands of P. aeruginosa genomes from the Bakta database that were tested for different antibiotics, and trained classifiers to predict the resistance phenotype using these embeddings. By training a Positive-Unlabeled classifier on genomic windows, where windows from susceptible strains were labeled positive and windows from resistant strains were unlabeled, I was able to identify the windows harboring the relevant genes in the resistant strains.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
