/T (Impact of Structural Variants on Genome and Proteome Evolution in Saccharomyces cerevisiae)
/E (Impact of Structural Variants on Genome and Proteome Evolution in Saccharomyces cerevisiae)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 164 0 R
/T (               Structural Variants \(SV\) are genomic rearrangements affecting DNA on a large scale. By altering gene dosage, disrupting coding sequences, or creating gene fusions, SVs affect gene function. Yet their functional and evolutionary impacts remain poorly understood. Leveraging the S. cerevisiae Reference Assembly Panel \(ScRAP\) pangenome and our upgraded SV-caller MUM&Co, we comprehensively identified SVs in the species population. By integrating them into the pangenome annotation we will assess their impact on gene repertoire evolution. Combining strain-specific annotations and proteomics data will reveal the effects of SVs on protein abundance and uncover SV-derived proteins. Structural properties of newly emerged protein domains will highlight SVs contribution to protein folds evolution. Ultimately, associations between SVs and phenotypic traits will be tested to provide insight into their adaptive significance.)
/E (               Structural Variants \(SV\) are genomic rearrangements affecting DNA on a large scale. By altering gene dosage, disrupting coding sequences, or creating gene fusions, SVs affect gene function. Yet their functional and evolutionary impacts remain poorly understood. Leveraging the S. cerevisiae Reference Assembly Panel \(ScRAP\) pangenome and our upgraded SV-caller MUM&Co, we comprehensively identified SVs in the species population. By integrating them into the pangenome annotation we will assess their impact on gene repertoire evolution. Combining strain-specific annotations and proteomics data will reveal the effects of SVs on protein abundance and uncover SV-derived proteins. Structural properties of newly emerged protein domains will highlight SVs contribution to protein folds evolution. Ultimately, associations between SVs and phenotypic traits will be tested to provide insight into their adaptive significance.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
