2583 0 obj
<<
/Title (Protein Quantitative Trait Loci \(pQTLs\) link genetic variation and disease biology. However, standard studies lack statistical power to detect distal \(trans\)-pQTLs, and current recovery methods rely on prior knowledge. Here, we present a strategy to uncover trans-pQTLs via "co-pleiotropy": the overlap of proteins associated with variant pairs. Applying Rank-Rank Hypergeometric Overlap \(RRHO\) to the UKB-PPP and deCODE datasets, we compared variant association profiles to identify functional protein clusters without a priori assumptions. By aggregating subtle signals, we detect biologically relevant sub-threshold variants that recapitulate known protein-protein interaction networks. This framework offers a powerful tool to decode the post-transcriptional landscape of GWAS loci.)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2572 0 R
>>
endobj
2584 0 obj
<<
/Title (Emergence of antibiotic resistant bacteria is a pressing issue in modern day medicine. To fight it and develop new drugs for new resistance mechanisms we have to detect the genetic cause of the resistance. Interpretable artificial intelligence models that predict the resistance phenotype from the full genome sequences are ideal for the task. To train these models, I use a genomic LLM, Evo, to produce embeddings from the genomic sequences. I embedded thousands of P. aeruginosa genomes from the Bakta database that were tested for different antibiotics, and trained classifiers to predict the resistance phenotype using these embeddings. By training a Positive-Unlabeled classifier on genomic windows, where windows from susceptible strains were labeled positive and windows from resistant strains were unlabeled, I was able to identify the windows harboring the relevant genes in the resistant strains.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2573 0 R
>>
endobj
